• The probe is conclusive:

  1. Parse sets: dropping the whitelist only adds junk pairs `('---','---')` (separator row) and `('CLASS','surface')` (header row) — these can never match a lookup key because coverage is matched by exact `(declared_class, surface)` at `is_covered = (cls, key) in covered` (accept-ca.py:251), and every declared class is in the whitelist.

  2. Wrong-class fixture: ORIGINAL and WHITELIST-DROPPED produce byte-identical verdicts — both FAIL, both reject wrong-class rows. No false accept.

  3. The mutation is verdict-neutral. The whitelist is redundant defense-in-depth: the class axis is enforced at lookup, not at parse.

  Residual observations worth recording:
  - The junk pairs are harmless but noisy; if a future claims.json declared a class outside the 3-name tuple, the ORIGINAL parser would silently ignore that class's correction rows (parse gate rejects → coverage impossible → claim unfixable by design), while the mutated parser would cover it. I.e., the whitelist couples the parser to the claims taxonomy; a claims-declared-but-unknown class would make an unfixable claim under the original. That's a coupling observation, not a defect observed here (all 5 declared pairs use whitelisted classes — verified: LEGCOUNT×3, DEADPOINTER×1, OVERREACH×1).
  - Do-nothing control: still bites (FAIL as required) — identical to the pre-data control receipt (controls.txt: GREEN 8/8, DO-NOTHING FAIL 2/8). But the control is blind to parser mutations of this class: it only tests "corrections file entirely absent". It cannot distinguish the mutated parser from the original — demonstrated by identical selftest output. The control that WOULD bite on a genuine coverage-forgiveness mutation is the wrong-class fixture (a third control: corrections present but non-conforming must FAIL) — and the counter passes that one too, so no gap remains on this axis.

  Also the real-tree run: ACCEPT-CA-69: FAIL legs=2/8, unannotated_mismatches=5 — all five declared pairs live and uncovered, exactly the pre-data state the brief describes (corrections-r68.md absent, verified: ls → No such file). R68FROZEN PASS (a6d1c1c24e77 both), OBSERVED PASS (o-legs=(8,9), ca-verdict=FAIL — the R68-M9 crash receipt). Counter bites on the real tree.

  One more sanity check for the report: the divergence hunt — is there ANY input where orig and mut differ? Formally: verdict differs only if some (row_class.upper(), row_surface) ∈ added-junk matches a lookup (cls, key). Lookup keys have cls ∈ {LEGCOUNT, DEADPOINTER, OVERREACH}; junk row classes are arbitrary strings from rows... wait, no — the "added" set isn't limited to header/separator; ANY row with a non-whitelisted class gets added, e.g. "| LEGCOUNT " (with trailing space → "LEGCOUNT" after strip — that's whitelisted). What about a row "| LEGCOUNT |" where surface empty → skipped. Hmm, what about case: "| deadpointer |" → upper → DEADPOINTER — whitelisted already. So a non-whitelisted class string can never equal a whitelisted class string after .upper(). QED: verdict-neutral for any corrections content, given claims classes ⊆ whitelist. The only divergence scenario is claims declaring a non-whitelisted class (not the case here; and if it were, mut would accept rows the orig ignores). I'll state this as a proved invariant with the strip/upper reasoning, plus the empirical parse-set diff.

